{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789820689,"nonce":"cOyKs37ovRUmJ6a29qRtx6T2PQE=","title":"SSA and phi_prune leave dead values in the table; the interpreter ran out of registers","message":"Construction creates an undef per (variable, block) read where no\ndefinition reaches, and the exit block's IDF phis read every variable\nfrom every raise path. A 160-line module body had 59k undef values in\nthe table, 45k unreferenced, and 278 phis of 93 incomings at the exit\nblock that phi_prune removed while leaving their results and undef\nincomings in the table. The interpreter allocates a register per value\n(`Reg = u16`) and panicked with \"register overflow\" at 66k; Cranelift\nallocated a 24-byte stack slot per undefined aggregate, 528 KB of frame.\n\nFix: `HirFunction::sweep_unreferenced_values` drops every value no\nparameter, instruction, phi or terminator names, run by `SsaBuilder::finish`\nand after phi_prune; scalar undefs are shared per type in construction\n(an aggregate undef stays its own, it is storage). The module body came\ndown to 27k values before optimisation.\n\nStill owed: the interpreter's u16 register limit is a hard failure with\nno fallback. A function too large for it should be compiled at tier 0 and\ncalled natively instead, the way an instruction it cannot run already is.","files":null}]}